Astrogriot Polyphony
Ingredients: AFROBEAT + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Djembe
Creative Catalyst: Musical instruments forged from extraterrestrial meteorite fragments and harvested 18th-century antique clockwork mechanisms.

Astrogriot Polyphony is a dizzying, maximalist collision of militant Afrobeat horn arrangements, hallucinatory tape manipulations, and manic West African polyrhythms mechanically driven by gears and cosmic iron. At its core, hand-carved djembes are retrofitted with ticking brass escapements and lined with powdered pallasite meteorite, producing sub-bass thumps that bloom into unnatural, bell-like metallic overtones that sustain far past physical possibility. Interlocking rhythm guitars clatter with the microscopic precision of Swiss watch pendulums while overdriven horns smear across cavernous tape delays, generating a dense, sweat-drenched dance ritual suspended between 1970s Lagos and an unmapped starfield.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 112–126 BPM. Relentless, hypnotic 12/8 cross-rhythms superimposed over an accelerating 4/4 Afrobeat kick-and-snare strut, driven by the manic micro-timing friction of antique clockwork tourbillons ticking in polyrhythmic opposition.
- Harmonic Palette: Modal Dorian and Mixolydian vamps spiked with eerie microtonal dissonances produced by struck siderite chimes; acidic, wah-drenched brass clusters and organ drones anchored by droning extraterrestrial root notes.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Horological Spring Flutter: Aux-sends routed through 18th-century brass carriage-clock casings to produce metallic, fluttering mechanical slapback delays.
  2. Meteoritic Resonance Gating: Direct-contact microphones clamped to meteorite-bolted djembe bowls, gating low-end transients so each skin strike triggers a shimmering tail of cosmic mineral reverb.
  3. Dual-Pendulum Stereo Panning: Hard-panned interlocking rhythm guitars and shekeres running through bespoke analog tremolos clocked to physical mechanical pendulums swinging at discordant speeds.
- Dynamics: Extended, trance-inducing ten-to-fifteen-minute builds that simmer with ticking cogs and hypnotic bass ostinatos before erupting into blistering horn polyphonies, then plummeting without warning into a solitary, ticking djembe heartbeat.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement materialized during the torrential dry-season humidity of Abeokuta in 2029, born in the clandestine basement workshops of the Kalakuta Horological Guild. A circle of dissident percussionists, watchmakers, and herbalists recovered shattered fragments of the historic 1962 Gashua chondrite meteorite and began riveting the extraterrestrial ore directly into the balances, gear-trains, and counter-weights of salvage grandfather clocks. By mounting these ticking assemblies inside the hollow bodies of goatskin djembes, they unleashed an acoustic engine that mesmerized listeners into waking trances. Guild fashion reflects this alchemical collision: musicians wear floor-length, wax-print dashikis tailored with jeweler's aprons, brass loupes strapped over one eye, and forehead markings ground from magnetic meteorite powder. Their all-night, illicit dance gatherings—held in defunct Victorian botanical greenhouses beneath humming ultraviolet grow-lights—are treated as communal temporal recalibrations where hundreds dance in lockstep until the clock springs wind down.

Typical Instruments
- Chondrite-Escapement Djembe: A carved iroko-wood djembe lined with pulverized meteorite dust and fitted with internal 18th-century clockwork escapements that tick in rapid polyrhythms with every skin strike.
- Siderite Brass Section: Trumpets and tenor saxophones lacquered in meteorite filings, utilizing antique clock-gear plungers as harmonic mutes.
- Horometer Clavinet: A vintage D6 clavinet equipped with mechanical pendulum-driven magnetic pickups that flutter continuously.
- Pallasite Shekere: Traditional dried gourds beaded with extraterrestrial olivine-peridot crystals rather than shells.
- Escapement Rhythm Guitars: Clean, dual electric guitars played in hyper-syncopated claw-hammer styles to simulate interlocking watch gears.

